[2. Today’s Topic]
The JwtAuthenticationFilter
[3. Concept Explanation]
Right now, if you try to access a protected page, Spring Security blocks you. It doesn't know you have a token in your pocket.
We need to build a Filter.
A Filter is a piece of code that runs before every single request hits your Controller.
The Analogy: The Airport Security Checkpoint
 * Login (AuthController): The Check-in Counter. You showed ID, they gave you a Boarding Pass (JWT).
 * The Filter (JwtAuthenticationFilter): The TSA Officer standing before the gates.
   * Step 1: You walk up. Officer says: "Show me your Boarding Pass." (Check Header).
   * Step 2: Officer scans it. "Is this fake? Is it expired?" (Validate Token).
   * Step 3: Officer looks at the name. "Okay, Adesh, go through." (Set SecurityContext).
If you don't have this Filter, the Officer (Spring Security) will just ask for your password again, ignoring the pass in your hand.
[4. The Code Implementation]
Create a new class JwtAuthenticationFilter.java.
Note: This code looks long, but it is just a simple "If-This-Then-That" checklist.
package org.adesh.security1.Config; // Put this in Config or Filter package

import jakarta.servlet.FilterChain;
import jakarta.servlet.ServletException;
import jakarta.servlet.http.HttpServletRequest;
import jakarta.servlet.http.HttpServletResponse;
import org.adesh.security1.Service.JwtService;
import org.adesh.security1.Service.CustomUserDetailsService;
import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Autowired;
import org.springframework.security.authentication.UsernamePasswordAuthenticationToken;
import org.springframework.security.core.context.SecurityContextHolder;
import org.springframework.security.core.userdetails.UserDetails;
import org.springframework.security.web.authentication.WebAuthenticationDetailsSource;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;
import org.springframework.web.filter.OncePerRequestFilter;

import java.io.IOException;

@Component
public class JwtAuthenticationFilter extends OncePerRequestFilter {

    @Autowired
    private JwtService jwtService; // The Scanner Tool

    @Autowired
    private CustomUserDetailsService userDetailsService; // To load full user data

    @Override
    protected void doFilterInternal(HttpServletRequest request, 
                                    HttpServletResponse response, 
                                    FilterChain filterChain) throws ServletException, IOException {

        // Step 1: Get the Token from the Header
        // The client sends: "Authorization: Bearer eyJhbGci..."
        final String authHeader = request.getHeader("Authorization");
        final String jwt;
        final String username;

        // Check: Is header missing? Does it start with "Bearer "?
        if (authHeader == null || !authHeader.startsWith("Bearer ")) {
            filterChain.doFilter(request, response); // Pass the request to the next filter (Login page usually)
            return;
        }

        // Extract the token (Remove "Bearer " prefix)
        jwt = authHeader.substring(7);
        
        // Step 2: Extract Username from Token
        username = jwtService.getUsernameFromToken(jwt); // "adesh"

        // Step 3: Check if user is not already logged in
        // (SecurityContextHolder.getContext().getAuthentication() == null) means "No one is logged in yet"
        if (username != null && SecurityContextHolder.getContext().getAuthentication() == null) {
            
            // Load the full user details from DB
            UserDetails userDetails = this.userDetailsService.loadUserByUsername(username);

            // Step 4: Validate the Token
            if (jwtService.isTokenValid(jwt)) { // Check signature & expiration
                
                // Step 5: CREATE THE SECURITY BADGE
                // We create a UsernamePasswordAuthenticationToken manually.
                UsernamePasswordAuthenticationToken authToken = new UsernamePasswordAuthenticationToken(
                        userDetails,
                        null,
                        userDetails.getAuthorities()
                );

                authToken.setDetails(new WebAuthenticationDetailsSource().buildDetails(request));

                // Step 6: UPDATE THE SECURITY CONTEXT
                // This is the Magic Line. We tell Spring: "This user is verified. Let them pass."
                SecurityContextHolder.getContext().setAuthentication(authToken);
            }
        }
        
        // Step 7: Continue the journey
        filterChain.doFilter(request, response);
    }
}

[5. Line-by-Line Explanation]
Line: extends OncePerRequestFilter
 * Meaning: "Please run this code exactly one time for every request."
Step 1 (The Header Check):
 * request.getHeader("Authorization"): We look for the specific header where tokens live.
 * !authHeader.startsWith("Bearer "): Standard convention. Tokens always start with the word "Bearer ". If it's missing, we ignore this request.
Step 2 (Extraction):
 * jwtService.getUsernameFromToken(jwt): We use the JwtService method you pasted earlier. It opens the token payload and reads "sub": "adesh".
Step 6 (The Magic Line):
 * SecurityContextHolder.getContext().setAuthentication(authToken):
   * Before this line: Spring thinks you are an anonymous stranger.
   * After this line: Spring knows you are "Adesh", and you are Authenticated.
   * This effectively logs you in for the duration of this one request (milliseconds).
[6. CRITICAL FINAL STEP: Register the Filter]
You wrote the code, but you didn't plug it in. You must add this filter to your SecurityConfig.
Update SecurityConfig.java:
@Configuration
@EnableWebSecurity
public class SecurityConfig {

    @Autowired
    private JwtAuthenticationFilter jwtAuthenticationFilter; // Inject your new filter

    @Bean
    public SecurityFilterChain securityFilterChain(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
        http
            .csrf(csrf -> csrf.disable())
            .authorizeHttpRequests(auth -> auth
                .requestMatchers("/api/auth/**").permitAll() // Login/Register is Open
                .anyRequest().authenticated()                // Everything else is Locked
            )
            .sessionManagement(sess -> sess.sessionCreationPolicy(SessionCreationPolicy.STATELESS)) // New! No Sessions.
            .authenticationProvider(authenticationProvider()) // Your DaoAuth provider
            
            // PLUG IN THE FILTER HERE:
            .addFilterBefore(jwtAuthenticationFilter, UsernamePasswordAuthenticationFilter.class); 

        return http.build();
    }
    
    // ... (Your other beans like passwordEncoder, authenticationManager remain here) ...
}

 * addFilterBefore(...): This tells Spring: "Run my JWT Filter BEFORE you run the standard Username/Password Filter." This gives our token a chance to work first.
 * sessionCreationPolicy(SessionCreationPolicy.STATELESS): Tells Spring: "Do not create a server-side session (JSESSIONID). We are using tokens now."
[7. Testing Flow]
 * Login: Send POST /login. Copy the Token.
 * Access: Create a new GET request to a secured endpoint (e.g., create a simple /api/demo controller).
 * Header: In Postman, go to the Headers tab (or Auth tab -> Bearer Token).
   * Key: Authorization
   * Value: Bearer eyJhbGci... (Paste your token).
 * Send: You should see 200 OK.
 * Test Failure: Remove the last character of the token and send. You should get 403 Forbidden.
